THE APEX'S ROLE:
  The apex tile is processed FIRST in the cascade.
  It sets the COARSEST constraint: t ∈ [1/n, (n-1)/n].
  This is the polygon's OUTSIDE — the boundary minus one edge.
  All subsequent constraints operate INSIDE this boundary.

  The cascade is a REFINEMENT from outside to inside:
  Step 1 (apex, speed 1): the coarsest constraint, width (n-2)/n
  Step 2 (speed 2): finer, granularity 1/(2n)
  Step k (speed k): finest, granularity 1/(kn)

  The LAST step (speed n-1) is the finest constraint.
  It corresponds to the SHORTEST diagonal in the polygon
  (connecting near-adjacent vertices).

  OUTSIDE → INSIDE = COARSE → FINE = SLOW → FAST
  The cascade refines from the polygon boundary to the interior.

======================================================================
PART 5: The Hamiltonian cycle — apex anti-aligned
======================================================================

When the APEX is anti-aligned (1 → n, sink beats source):
  The polygon boundary forms a HAMILTONIAN CYCLE:
  n → n-1 → ... → 2 → 1 → n
  (base path: n→...→1, plus apex: 1→n)

  Every vertex reaches every other via the cycle.
  The tournament is 'maximally cyclic' at the boundary level.
  H is MAXIMIZED for this boundary (the cycle contributes
  many paths that wrap around).

When the APEX is aligned (n → 1, source beats sink):
  The boundary is a DIRECTED PATH (no cycle):
  n → n-1 → ... → 1, and n → 1
  The source n beats ALL others via the path + direct arc.
  The tournament is 'maximally transitive' at the boundary level.
  H is MINIMIZED for this boundary (only one path: the base path).

THE LRC TRANSITION:
  At t < 1/n: runner 1 is close → apex ANTI-ALIGNED → cyclic boundary
  At t = 1/n: runner 1 crosses threshold → apex FLIPS → transitive boundary
  At t > 1/n: runner 1 is safe → apex ALIGNED → observer can be source

  The FIRST event in the cascade (apex flipping at t=1/n) is the
  TRANSITION from cyclic to transitive boundary structure.
  All subsequent cascade steps happen WITHIN the transitive boundary.

  This is why the initial segment has its lonely time at t=1/n:
  it's exactly when the apex flips, opening the transitive window.

======================================================================
SYNTHESIS: The apex closes the polygon
======================================================================

The regular polygon has n boundary edges:
  n-1 BASE PATH edges (the ranking)
  1 APEX TILE (closing the cycle)

The apex tile is the MOST IMPORTANT tile because:
  1. It determines the global topology (path vs cycle)
  2. It connects the two extremes (source to sink)
  3. It's the FIRST constraint in the cascade (coarsest)
  4. Its flip at t=1/n opens the transitive window
  5. Its H-impact is among the largest of all tiles

For LRC: the apex = 'is the slowest runner safe?'
  When the apex flips from anti-aligned (cyclic) to aligned
  (transitive), the observer's source potential opens up.
  The remaining cascade steps check the interior tiles.

THE PICTURE:
  t < 1/n: apex CLOSED → cyclic boundary → observer blocked
  t = 1/n: apex OPENS → transition to transitive boundary
  t > 1/n: apex OPEN → cascade checks interior → lonely or not

  The polygon's OUTSIDE (base path + apex) sets the STAGE.
  The polygon's INSIDE (diagonals) determines the OUTCOME.
  The apex is the GATE between these two worlds.

